What problem does this project solve?,overview,"['--- Source 1 ---\nFile: README.md\nType: readme\nPriority: 1\nContent:\n192: \n193: **Step 13 — Merge & Deduplicate**\n194: The retrieved documents are merged and deduplicated.\n195: \n196: **Step 14 — Reranking**\n197: Each surviving chunk is scored by a **cross-encoder reranker** against the (rewritten) question, producing a `reranker_score`. This re-orders results by actual relevance rather than raw vector-similarity alone, correcting cases where embedding search alone would surface a superficially similar but less useful chunk.\n198: \n199: **Step 15 — Priority-Aware Sorting**\n200: The reranked documents are sorted using a combination of reranker score and file priority before being passed to the LLM.\n201: \n202: **Step 16 — Answer Generation**\n203: The Groq LLM (Llama 3.3 70B Versatile) generates a response using only the retrieved, reranked repository context, and returns a source-aware answer.\n204: \n205: ---\n206: \n207: ## ✨ Example Questions\n208: \n209: **Overview**\n210: - What is Flask?\n211: - What problem does this project solve?\n212: - Explain this project.\n213: \n214: **Architecture**\n215: - Explain the project architecture.\n216: - Explain the folder structure.\n217: - What are the main modules?\n218: \n219: **Implementation**', '--- Source 2 ---\nFile: README.md\nType: readme\nPriority: 1\nContent:\n215: - Explain the project architecture.\n216: - Explain the folder structure.\n217: - What are the main modules?\n218: \n219: **Implementation**\n220: - How are routes implemented?\n221: - Where is Session defined?\n222: - Explain this function.\n223: \n224: **Configuration**\n225: - How is the project configured?\n226: - Where are environment variables defined?\n227: \n228: **Dependency**\n229: - Which libraries are required?\n230: - What packages does this project use?\n231: \n232: ---\n233: \n234: ## 🌟 Key Features\n235: \n236: - Conversational RAG\n237: - Semantic Search\n238: - LLM-based Query Routing\n239: - Multi-Retriever Architecture\n240: - **Cross-Encoder Reranking**\n241: - Repository Structure Generation\n242: - Source-aware Responses\n243: - Metadata-aware Retrieval\n244: - Duplicate Removal\n245: - Priority + Relevance-based Context Selection\n246: \n247: ---\n248: \n249: ## 📈 Future Enhancements\n250: \n251: - Parent-Child Retrieval\n252: - Hybrid Search (BM25 + Vector Search)\n253: - Repository Summarization\n254: - Mermaid Architecture Diagram Generation\n255: - Cross-file Execution Tracing\n256: - Multi-Agent Code Analysis\n257: - Support for Private GitHub Repositories', '--- Source 3 ---\nFile: README.md\nType: readme\nPriority: 1\nContent:\n255: - Cross-file Execution Tracing\n256: - Multi-Agent Code Analysis\n257: - Support for Private GitHub Repositories\n258: - Code Review and Bug Detection\n259: \n260: ---\n261: \n262: ## 👨\u200d💻 Author\n263: \n264: **Lakshmi Charan Yakkala**\n265: - GitHub: https://github.com/lakshmicharan18\n266: - LinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/in/charan-yakkala-95bbb8318/\n267: \n268: ---\n269: \n270: ## 📄 License\n271: \n272: This project is intended for educational and learning purposes.', '--- Source 4 ---\nFile: README.md\nType: readme\nPriority: 1\nContent:\n1: # 💻 GitHub Codebase Assistant\n2: \n3: An intelligent Conversational Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) application that allows users to ask natural language questions about any public GitHub repository. The application clones a repository, indexes its source code and documentation, retrieves and **reranks** the most relevant chunks, and provides accurate, source-grounded answers using Large Language Models (LLMs).\n4: \n5: This was developed by **YAKKALA LAKSHMI CHARAN**, a student from RGUKT Nuzvid.\n6: \n7: ---\n8: \n9: ## 🚀 Features\n10: \n11: - Clone and process any public GitHub repository\n12: - Intelligent code and documentation indexing\n13: - Code-aware and documentation-aware chunking\n14: - Hugging Face Embeddings for semantic search\n15: - ChromaDB Vector Database\n16: - LLM-powered Query Routing\n17: - Conversational Question Rewriting\n18: - Multi-Retriever Architecture\n19: - **Cross-Encoder Reranking of retrieved chunks**\n20: - Auto-generated Repository Structure\n21: - Source-aware Answers with File References\n22: - Interactive Streamlit Chat Interface\n23: \n24: ---\n25: \n26: ## 🏗️ System Architecture\n27: \n28: ```\n29:             GitHub Repository URL\n30:                       │', '--- Source 5 ---\nFile: CLAUDE.md\nType: documentation\nPriority: 3\nContent:\n41: \n42: **Question flow before the RAG chain even runs:** `app.py` first calls `utils/question_rewriter.py`\'s `rewrite_question()` (turns conversational follow-ups like ""explain that simply"" into standalone questions, using the last 6 chat messages — no-ops if there\'s no real history) and then passes the rewritten question into `create_rag_chain()`. Inside `create_rag_chain` (`utils/rag_chain.py`), the question is routed to a category (`utils/query_router.py`), multi-retrieved (`utils/multi_retriever.py`, capped at 20 docs), deduplicated, then reranked by a cross-encoder (`utils/reranker.py`, cached process-wide via `st.cache_resource`, top 6 survive) before being stuffed into the final answer-generation prompt. Both the embedding model and reranker model are `@st.cache_resource`-cached at module scope — loading them is expensive, and this cache is what makes multiple sessions share one copy instead of reloading weights per session.\n43: \n44: **License retrieval has a fallback**: `retrieve_license()` in `multi_retriever.py` falls back to README chunks if no dedicated LICENSE file matched, since many repos state their license only in the README.\n45:', '--- Source 6 ---\nFile: README.md\nType: readme\nPriority: 1\nContent:\n61:      ┌──────────┬──────────┬──────────┐\n62:      │          │          │          │\n63:      ▼          ▼          ▼          ▼\n64:  README      Source     Docs      Repo Structure\n65: Retriever   Retriever Retriever    Retriever\n66:      │          │          │          │\n67:      └──────────┴──────────┴──────────┘\n68:                       │\n69:                       ▼\n70:           Merge Retrieved Documents\n71:                       │\n72:                       ▼\n73:           Remove Duplicate Chunks\n74:                       │\n75:                       ▼\n76:         Cross-Encoder Reranking\n77:                       │\n78:                       ▼\n79:             Sort by Reranker Score\n80:              (with File Priority)\n81:                       │\n82:                       ▼\n83:            Groq LLM Generates Answer\n84:                       │\n85:                       ▼\n86:           Answer with Source Citations\n87: ```\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 📂 Project Structure\n92: \n93: ```\n94: github-codebase-assistant/\n95: │\n96: ├── app.py\n97: │\n98: ├── utils/\n99: │   ├── repo_loader.py\n100: │   ├── code_splitter.py\n101: │   ├── vector_store.py\n102: │   ├── query_router.py']","The project tackles the difficulty of understanding and navigating large public GitHub repositories. It provides an **intelligent conversational RAG (Retrieval‑Augmented Generation) assistant** that can clone any repository, index its code and documentation, retrieve the most relevant pieces, rerank them, and then answer natural‑language questions with source‑grounded responses — so users can ask “What does this project do?” or “Explain this function?” without manually searching through the code 【README.md†L1-L4】.",0.249999999975,,0.659689667614716
